{"project_name": "VUB Publications Management System", "initial_requirements": "Vrije Universiteit Brussel needs a Publications Management System for storing bibliographic records and full publication texts. Users with different roles should be able to search, upload, download, correct, and moderate publication information according to their access rights. The system should extract bibliographic details from documents, support bulk imports, and help reconcile alternative names that refer to the same author or institution.", "messages": [{"message_id": "M001", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Hello, I’m GPTREI, your requirements elicitation assistant. I’ll be gathering detailed information about the VUB Publications Management System so we can understand the needs, workflows, and priorities clearly.

Before we begin, are you aligned with the scope of this interview, or do your expectations differ?"}, {"message_id": "M002", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Yes, that sounds aligned with what we need. I’m here to discuss the publications management system for VUB, especially how people store, search, upload, correct, and moderate publication records and full texts, so we can clarify the workflow and access rights."}, {"message_id": "M003", "role": "interviewer", "content": "To start off, could you please introduce yourself and your connection to this project?"}, {"message_id": "M004", "role": "interviewee", "content": "I’m speaking here as someone representing the VUB side of the publications management needs, so I’m familiar with how our library, research support, and publishing workflows are supposed to work. My focus is on making sure the system fits the daily processes for handling bibliographic records, publication files, and author/institution name reconciliation."}, {"message_id": "M005", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Ok, now that I know the general goal, let's talk a bit about the systems and workflows you currently use. Can you describe the current process or system you use for managing publication records and full texts?"}, {"message_id": "M006", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Right now it’s handled across a few different places, so it’s not as integrated as we’d like. Bibliographic data often comes from manual entry or imports, and full texts are stored separately or exchanged by email and shared folders, with staff checking and correcting records by hand."}, {"message_id": "M007", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Ok, what current processes do you want to change or improve, or what are the main features you hope to get from this project?"}, {"message_id": "M008", "role": "interviewee", "content": "We want to bring the bibliographic records and the full texts into one managed system, with less manual work and fewer inconsistencies. The biggest improvements are better search, easier upload and bulk import, automatic extraction of bibliographic details from documents, and a cleaner way to handle name variants for authors and institutions."}, {"message_id": "M009", "role": "interviewer", "content": "So far I have these main features/functions: unified storage for bibliographic records and full texts, improved search, upload and bulk import, automatic extraction of bibliographic details from documents, and reconciliation of alternative names for authors and institutions. Is that correct?"}, {"message_id": "M010", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Yes, that captures the core of it well. I’d also add that different user roles need different permissions for searching, uploading, correcting, downloading, and moderating, because not everyone should be able to do everything."}, {"message_id": "M011", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Based on your description, do you think support for role-based access rights and permissions should also be included as a core feature?"}, {"message_id": "M012", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Yes, definitely. That’s not just a nice-to-have for us; it’s essential because the system will be used by different groups, and we need to control who can edit, approve, or only view and download records."}, {"message_id": "M013", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Could you please prioritize these main features for me, starting with the most important one?"}, {"message_id": "M014", "role": "interviewee", "content": "The most important is the role-based access and moderation, because without that we can’t safely let multiple groups work in the same system. After that, I’d put unified storage for bibliographic records and full texts, then search, then upload and bulk import, then automatic extraction, and finally the name reconciliation piece, although that one is still quite important for data quality."}, {"message_id": "M015", "role": "interviewer", "content": "For the highest-priority feature, role-based access and moderation, could you describe what different roles you expect in the system and what each role should be allowed to do?"}, {"message_id": "M016", "role": "interviewee", "content": "We’d expect at least a basic separation between regular users who can search and maybe upload, editors or staff who can correct records, and moderators who approve or reject changes before they become official. Some users should probably only have read access, while others can download full texts or manage imports, but I’d need to check the exact role names with the team."}, {"message_id": "M017", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Could you describe what approval or rejection of changes should look like for moderators, step by step?"}, {"message_id": "M018", "role": "interviewee", "content": "When a change comes in, it should sit in a pending state until a moderator reviews it. The moderator should be able to compare the proposed change with the existing record, approve it so it becomes visible, or reject it with a reason so the contributor knows what needs fixing."}, {"message_id": "M019", "role": "interviewer", "content": "Thank you. For role-based access and moderation, do you already have a current system or workflow for approvals and permissions, or would this be entirely new?"}, {"message_id": "M020", "role": "interviewee", "content": "We do have some current practices, but they’re not really in one proper system, and a lot of it is handled manually or across different tools. So this would be more of a new centralized workflow than just a small upgrade of something existing."}, {"message_id": "M021", "role": "interviewer", "content": "What other stakeholders do you think have valuable insights into this project, and whose needs should also be considered?"}, {"message_id": "M022", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Definitely the library staff and the people managing institutional repositories, because they deal with the publication data day to day. Researchers and department administrators are also important, since they submit or use the records, and IT or system administrators should be involved because they’ll care about integration, security, and maintenance."}, {"message_id": "M023", "role": "interviewer", "content": "What would you consider a successful deployment of this system?"}, {"message_id": "M024", "role": "interviewee", "content": "A successful deployment would mean people can reliably search, upload, and manage publication records in one place without depending on manual workarounds. It should also reduce errors in author and institution names, and the approval workflow should be clear enough that staff can trust the data."}, {"message_id": "M025", "role": "interviewer", "content": "To summarize, we discussed a centralized Publications Management System for VUB with role-based access and moderation as the top priority, followed by unified storage, search, upload/bulk import, automatic bibliographic extraction, and name reconciliation. We also identified library staff, repository managers, researchers, department administrators, and IT/system administrators as key stakeholders, and success means reliable one-place management with reduced manual work and fewer data errors.

Is there anything else you would like to add?"}, {"message_id": "M026", "role": "interviewee", "content": "Yes, one important point is that the system should fit into existing VUB workflows as much as possible, especially around repository processes and any current import sources. It would also be good if the interface is straightforward enough that occasional users can use it without much training."}]}
